h \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n14. **Inequality from the bound**\n\n    > “If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (2.5) yields  \n    > \\[\n    > k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n    > \\tag{3.2}\n    > \\]”\n\n    *Correct.*  Multiplying the per‑line bound by the number of lines gives a necessary condition for covering all points.\n\n---\n\n15. **Even \\(k\\) impossible**\n\n    > “*Even \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then the left‑hand side of (3.2) equals\n    > \\(2m\\cdot m=m(2m)=k^{2}/2\\), whereas the right‑hand side equals\n    > \\(m(2m+1)=k^{2}/2+m\\).  Because \\(m>0\\), (3.2) is false.  Hence **no even\n    > \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**.”\n\n    *Correct.*  Straightforward algebra shows the inequality fails for any even \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n---\n\n16. **Odd \\(k\\) gives equality**\n\n    > “*Odd \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\)\n    > and (3.2) becomes an equality:\n    > \\[\n    > (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 .\n    > \\tag{3.3}\n    > \\]  \n    > Consequently every sunny line must contain **exactly** \\(m+1\\) points of\n    > \\(T_{2m+1}\\), and the \\(k\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.”\n\n    *Correct.*  Equality forces each line to attain the maximal possible number of points, and the point‑sets must be disjoint.\n\n---\n\n17. **Identification of the “maximal” lines** *(first problematic passage)*\n\n    > “Equation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:\n    > * \\(p=q=1\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n    > * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\) (slope \\(-2\\));\n    > * \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).”\n\n    *Issue (Finding 1).*  The second and third possibilities have \\(|p+q|=1\\) (since \\(-2+1=-1\\) and \\(-1+2=1\\)).  The statement “with \\(|p+q|=2\\)” is therefore inaccurate.  The intended classification is that the **three families** (slope 1, slope –2, slope –½) are the only ones that can achieve the bound, but the wording is wrong.  This is a **Justification Gap** (presentation error) that does not affect the later correct use of the three families.\n\n---\n\n18. **Claim of uniqueness of the three lines**\n\n    > “For \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point‑sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.”\n\n    *Issue (Finding 3).*  The argument that **only one** line of each of the three families can contain \\(m+1\\) points is not made explicit.  In fact, for a given family the intercept is forced (e.g. for slope 1 we need the line \\(y=x\\); for slope –2 we need \\(y=-2x+2m+3\\); for slope –½ we need \\(x+2y=2m+3\\)).  The solution should state why any other intercept would reduce the number of points below \\(m+1\\).  The omission is a minor gap but does not invalidate the conclusion that at most three such lines exist.  Hence **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n19. **Deriving the final restriction on \\(k\\)**\n\n    > “Thus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only**\n    > the three lines (10).  Hence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\); i.e. \\(k=1\\) or\n    > \\(k=3\\).”\n\n    *Correct given the previous claim.*  Since we have at most three lines that can each contain the required \\(m+1\\) points, we cannot have more than three sunny lines.  Therefore the only admissible odd values are \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n---\n\n20. **Construction for \\(k=0\\)**\n\n    > “*\\(k=0\\).*  \n    > The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and cover\n    > every point of \\(S_n\\).”\n\n    *Correct.*  All points lie on some vertical line; no sunny line is used.\n\n---\n\n21. **Construction for \\(k=1\\)**\n\n    > “*\\(k=1\\).*  \n    > Use the 